#{[17592186203667 :question/worked-solution "Sets $A$ and $B$ have the *same cardinality* if there exists a bijection between $A$ and $B$.\n\nRecall that the function $y = \\tan x$ bijectively (i.e., both injectively and surjectively) maps the interval\n\n$$\n\\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}2,\\dfrac{\\pi}2\\right)\n$$\n\nonto the interval\n\n$$\n(-\\infty,\\infty) = \\mathbb{R}\n$$\n\n![](images/0c/0c30abace6b86aa63ce3da8bafa2bb4748271fdfa3d320ee0f2c7f18fbeec998.png)\n\nHowever, we need a function that maps from $(-3,3)$ onto $\\mathbb R$ instead of\n\n$$\n\\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}2,\\dfrac{\\pi}2\\right)\n$$\n\nonto $\\mathbb R$. To construct one, we can expand our tangent curve by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{6}{\\pi}\n$$\n\nalong the $x$-axis. This can be done by multiplying the argument of the function by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\n$$\n\nSo, we consider the function $f: A \\to B$ that maps the underlying sets as follows:\n\n$$\nf: x \\to \\tan \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi x}{6}\\right)\n$$\n\nNotice that $f$ bijectively maps $(-3,3)$ onto $\\mathbb{R}$.\n\n![](images/1e/1e8c3a10fc9688fcc009178d9777201d367e82ad0d9c410cc24fa925f5091e96.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|A| = |B|,\n$$\n\ni.e., the sets have the same cardinality."] [17592186203693 :question/worked-solution "For a relation $R$ defined over the sets $X$ and $Y$, and a relation $S$ defined over the sets $Y$ and $Z$, the *composition* $S \\circ R$ is given by\n\n$$\nS \\circ R = \\big\\{(x,z) \\in X \\times Z \\:: \\: \\exists y \\in Y, \\: x \\: R \\: y \\: \\land \\: y \\: S \\: z \\big\\}\n$$\n\nSince our relations are defined on relatively small sets, we can visualize them using the following mapping diagrams, where we match the elements of the two inner sets by the dashed lines:\n\n![](images/98/9819ec3233b22095898b87eb0f54de8adb8271a27e5a5d606fcce7b1c986a646.png)\n\nAn ordered pair belongs to the composition $S \\circ R$ if there is a path starting from the first component (on the left-hand side) and ending at the second component (on the right-hand side).\n\nIn our example, these paths are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & a⟶0\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{- - -}\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}0⟶{\\color{red}{\\times }} \\\\  & a⟶1\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{- - -}\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}1⟶4\\hspace{2em}\\hspace{2em} &  & \\text{Therefore, }(a,4)\\in S∘R \\\\  & a⟶1\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{- - -}\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}1⟶7\\hspace{2em}\\hspace{2em} &  & \\text{Therefore, }(a,7)\\in S∘R \\\\  & a⟶1\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{- - -}\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}1⟶10\\hspace{2em}\\hspace{2em} &  & \\text{Therefore, }(a,10)\\in S∘R \\\\  & c⟶0\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{- - -}\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}0⟶{\\color{red}{\\times }} \\\\  & d⟶1\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{- - -}\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}1⟶4\\hspace{2em}\\hspace{2em} &  & \\text{Therefore, }(d,4)\\in S∘R \\\\  & d⟶1\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{- - -}\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}1⟶7\\hspace{2em}\\hspace{2em} &  & \\text{Therefore, }(d,7)\\in S∘R \\\\  & d⟶1\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{- - -}\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}1⟶10\\hspace{2em}\\hspace{2em} &  & \\text{Therefore, }(d,10)\\in S∘R \\\\  & e⟶0\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{- - -}\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}0⟶{\\color{red}{\\times }} \\\\  & e⟶1\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{- - -}\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}1⟶4\\hspace{2em}\\hspace{2em} &  & \\text{Therefore, }(e,4)\\in S∘R \\\\  & e⟶1\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{- - -}\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}1⟶7\\hspace{2em}\\hspace{2em} &  & \\text{Therefore, }(e,7)\\in S∘R \\\\  & e⟶1\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{- - -}\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}1⟶10\\hspace{2em}\\hspace{2em} &  & \\text{Therefore, }(e,10)\\in S∘R\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, nine complete paths start on the left-hand side and end on the right-hand side. They correspond to the following ordered pairs:\n\n$$\nS \\circ R = \\big\\{(a,4), (a,7), (a,10), (d,4), (d,7), (d,10), (e,4), (e,7), (e,10)\\big\\}\n$$"] [17592186203721 :question/worked-solution "Sets $A$ and $B$ have the *same cardinality* if there exists a bijection of $A$ onto $B$.\n\nRecall that the function $y=x$ bijectively (i.e., both injectively and surjectively) maps the interval $(0,2)$ onto the interval $(0,2)$.\n\n![](images/8e/8ea2ae439c91dd7a6239b188ae90573511fcb533289d52ebfcd41b79f8b03242.png)\n\nHowever, we need a function that maps $(0,2)$ of length $2$ onto $(1,9)$ of length $8$. To construct one, we proceed as follows:\n\n- First, we stretch our function by a factor of $4$ along the $y$-axis, i.e, multiply the function by $4$.\n- Next, we shift the result $1$ units up, i.e., add $1$ to the result of the previous transformation.\n\nSo, we consider the function $f: (0,2) \\to (1,9)$ that maps the underlying sets as follows:\n\n$$\nf: x \\to 4x + 1\n$$\n\nNotice that $f$ bijectively maps $(0,2)$ onto $(1,9)$.\n\n![](images/cc/ccaf0cd63033f73a091a1d14188bc979c4090b1f94e61842917fd214552fb7ad.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|A| = |B|,\n$$\n\ni.e., the sets have the same cardinality."] [17592186203722 :question/worked-solution "Sets $A$ and $B$ have the *same cardinality* if there exists a bijection between $A$ and $B$.\n\nRecall that the function $y=e^x$ bijectively (i.e., both injectively and surjectively) maps the interval\n\n$$\n(-\\infty,\\infty) = \\mathbb{R}\n$$\n\nonto the interval $(0,\\infty)$.\n\n![](images/c0/c02ce2e0276f70672c2ee3873a48f68c42f53ea02450c03385d71a7c5257ed38.png)\n\nHowever, we need a function that maps $\\mathbb R$ onto $(-4,\\infty)$ instead of $(0,\\infty)$. To construct one, we shift our exponential graph by $4$ downward. This can be done by subtracting $4$ from the function.\n\nSo, we consider the function $f: B \\to A$ that maps the underlying sets as follows:\n\n$$\nf: x \\to e^{x}-4\n$$\n\nNotice that $f$ bijectively maps $\\mathbb{R}$ onto $(-4,\\infty)$.\n\n![](images/29/29d1bf44e638b8290eab5a08e14d7c16544d43152a5116f6fcb195698ddf47b4.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|A| = |B|,\n$$\n\ni.e., the sets have the same cardinality."] [17592186203732 :question/worked-solution "The connector \"if and only if\" means we need to prove an equivalence.\n\n$$\nP\\Leftrightarrow Q\n$$\n\nHowever, instead of proving an equivalence directly, we can instead prove two conditional statements.\n\n$$\nP\\Leftrightarrow Q \\equiv (P\\Rightarrow Q) \\land (Q\\Rightarrow P)\n$$\n\nSo, we start our proof as follows:\n\n> We need to show the following:\n> $\\textrm{n is even} \\quad\\Leftrightarrow\\quad \\textrm{{n}^{2} is even}$\n> This is the same as proving two implications.\n\nFirst, we consider the \"left to right\" implication.\n\n> **Case $\\Rightarrow{:}$** We need to show that\n> $\\textrm{n is even} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad \\textrm{{n}^{2} is even}.$\n> If $n$ is even, then $n \\equiv 0 \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:2)$. Therefore,\n> $\\begin{aligned}{n}^{2} & ≡{0}^{2} & (\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}2) \\\\  & ≡0 & (\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}2)\\end{aligned}$\n> which means that $n^2$ is even. So, the first implication is proved.\n\nNext, we consider the \"right to left\" implication.\n\n> **Case $\\Leftarrow{:}$** We need to show that\n> $$\n{ { {n}^{2}} \\textrm{ is even}} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad { {n} \\textrm{ is even}}.\n$$\n> Or, in terms of congruences,\n> $n^2 \\equiv 0 \\:\\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:2) \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad n \\equiv 0 \\:\\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:2)$.\n\nNow, the final statement can be re-written using negations as follows:\n\n> Since we're working modulo $2$ (where the only remainders are $0$ and $1$), this statement is equivalent to\n> $n^2 \\not\\equiv 1 \\:\\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:2) \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad n \\not\\equiv 1 \\:\\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:2)$.\n\nProving the negation statement can be a difficult task, but we can avoid that by using the contrapositive:\n\n> The contrapositive of the last statement is\n> $n \\equiv 1 \\:\\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:2) \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad n^2 \\equiv 1 \\:\\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:2)$.\n\nNext, we'll prove the contrapositive, as shown below.\n\n> Now, if $n \\equiv 1 \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:2)$, then\n> $\\begin{aligned}{n}^{2} & ≡{1}^{2} & (\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}2) \\\\  & ≡1 & (\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}2)\\end{aligned}$\n> as required. So, the second implication is proved.\n\nFinally, we write down the conclusion:\n\n> Therefore, $n$ is even if and only if $n^2$ is even."] [17592186205257 :tutorial/content "Recall that two sets $A$ and $B$ have the *same cardinality* if there exists a bijection of $A$ onto $B$. If no such bijection exists, the sets are said to have *unequal cardinalities*.\n\nIn this lesson, we'll discuss how bijections can be used to determine whether two real intervals have the same cardinality.\n\nTo begin, consider the intervals $A$ and $B$, defined below:\n\n$$\nA= (0,1), \\qquad B= (0,2)\n$$\n\nIn terms of their *lengths*, the second interval is twice as long as the first. But what about their cardinalities?\n\nSince the length of $B$ is twice that of $A$, our intuition might tell us that $B$ should have twice the cardinality of $A$. However, this is *not* the case! You might be surprised to learn that $A$ and $B$ have the same cardinality!\n\n$$\n|A| = |B|\n$$\n\nTo show why this is true, we need to construct a bijection that maps $A$ onto $B$.\n\nFirst, notice that the function $y=x$ bijectively (i.e., both injectively and surjectively) maps the interval $(0,1)$ onto the interval $(0,1)$.\n\n![](images/1a/1a1318e558e58556557556364fee6bdd53b1844a1e661a31e52138978841db37.png)\n\nHowever, we need a function that maps $(0,1)$ onto $(0,2)$ instead of onto $(0,1)$. To construct one, we can stretch our function by a factor of $2$ along the $y$-axis. This can be done by multiplying the function by $2$.\n\nSo, we consider the function $f: A \\to B$ that maps the underlying sets as follows:\n$f: x \\to 2x$\n\nNotice that $f$ bijectively maps $(0,1)$ onto $(0,2)$.\n\n![](images/81/81fca77700d1982091a68d32d548adbf1b2f4e734d7335f67d1e8684bc691a1c.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|A| = |B|,\n$$\n\ni.e., the sets have the same cardinality."] [17592186205258 :tutorial/content "As we've seen, transformations of the function\n\n$$\nf(x) = x\n$$\n\nhelp to construct bijections that map finite intervals to finite intervals. Let's now consider some functions we can use to map infinite intervals to infinite intervals.\n\n- The function $y=e^x$ bijectively (i.e., both injectively and surjectively) maps the interval $(-\\infty,\\infty) = \\mathbb{R}$ onto the interval $(0,\\infty)$.![](images/c0/c02ce2e0276f70672c2ee3873a48f68c42f53ea02450c03385d71a7c5257ed38.png)\n\n- On the other hand, $y=\\ln x$ bijectively maps the interval $(0,\\infty)$ onto the interval $(-\\infty,\\infty) = \\mathbb{R}$.![](images/84/84f0bcbc610b1f80dc592cde2f3e976d197c8f6e9de22f1586de0de73cc06843.png)\n\nIn particular, this means that\n\n$$\n\\big| (0,\\infty) \\big| = \\big| (-\\infty,\\infty) \\big| = |\\mathbb R|.\n$$\n\nSimilar to what we've seen previously, we can perform transformations on these elementary functions to construct bijections that map between different intervals. Let's see some examples."] [17592186205259 :tutorial/content "Finally, let's consider functions we can use to map an (open) segment to the real line and vice-versa.\n\n- The function $y = \\tan x$ bijectively (i.e., both injectively and surjectively) maps the interval $\\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}2,\\dfrac{\\pi}2\\right)$ onto the interval $(-\\infty,\\infty) = \\mathbb{R}$.![](images/0c/0c30abace6b86aa63ce3da8bafa2bb4748271fdfa3d320ee0f2c7f18fbeec998.png)\n- Similarly, the function $y = \\cot x$ bijectively maps the interval $\\left(0, \\pi\\right)$ onto the interval $(-\\infty,\\infty) = \\mathbb{R}$.![](images/8f/8f464060d171517b98dcf7cfd345420e534c349c2a98e83d2300f63f42a4132c.png)\n- Finally, the function $y = \\arctan x$ bijectively maps the interval $\\left(-\\infty, \\infty \\right)$ onto the interval $\\left(-\\dfrac\\pi2,\\dfrac\\pi2\\right)$.![](images/db/db7c994eeb92c6b2debd80129d65d20cf14dc039f9a3a3a46235f8c4054c720d.png)\n\nIn particular, this means that\n\n$$\n\\bigg| \\left( -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right) \\bigg| =\\big|(0,\\pi)\\big|= \\big| (-\\infty, \\infty) \\big| = \\big|\\mathbb R\\big|.\n$$"] [17592186208318 :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the given equation as\n$c^2 = 13^2$.\n\nNext, we take the square root of both sides of the equation and solve the resulting absolute value equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}^{2} & ={13}^{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{{c}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{{13}^{2}} \\\\ |c| & =|13| \\\\ |c| & =13 \\\\ c & =\\pm 13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the **negative** solution to the given equation is\n\n$$\nc = \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{-13}\n$$"] [17592186208391 :question/problem "$$\n3|4 - a| - 7|4 - a| = -60\n$$\nThe positive solution of the equation given above is $a =$"] [17592186208391 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the absolute value terms contain the same expression. So, by combining these like terms, the equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3|4-a|-7|4-a| & =-60 \\\\ -4|4-a| & =-60.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, dividing both sides by $-4$, we get\n\n$$\n\\mid 4 - a \\mid = 15\n$$\n\nNext, we consider both possible cases for the absolute value.\n\n- If $\\mid 4 - a \\mid = 4 - a$, then\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\mid 4 - a \\mid |= 15; 4 - a|= 15; - a|= 11; a|=-11.\n$$\n- If $\\mid 4 - a \\mid = -(4 - a)$, then\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\mid 4 - a \\mid |= 15; - (4 - a)|= 15; - 4 + a|= 15; a|= 19.\n$$\n\nSince the question asks for the positive solution, the correct answer is\n\n$$\na=\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{19}\n$$"] [17592186208402 :question/worked-solution "We are given the following system:\n\n$$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=49 \\\\ y &=x^{2} - 2x + 1\\end{aligned}\\right.$$\n\nSubstituting $y = 49$ into the second equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 2x + 1 &= 49 \\\\\nx^{2} - 2x + 1 - 49 &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2} - 2x - 48 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe solve this quadratic equation by factoring. We begin by finding two numbers that multiply to $-48$ and add to $-2$. These are $-8$ and $6{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 2x - 48 &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2}\\underbrace{-8x + 6x}_{-2x} - 48 &= 0 \\\\\nx(x - 8) + 6(x - 8) &= 0 \\\\\n(x - 8)(x + 6) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the zero product rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-8=0\\hspace{2em} & \\text{or}\\hspace{2em}x+6=0 \\\\ x=8\\hspace{2em} & \\text{or}\\hspace{2em}x=-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, among the given options, the possible value of $x$ is $8$."] [17592186208427 :question/worked-solution "We want to find all possible values of $y$ such that the point $(x, y)$ satisfies both inequalities.\n\nSince $x < -2$, the largest possible value of $5x - 3$, which serves as the upper bound for $y$, occurs as $x$ is near $-2$.\n\nTo identify this boundary value, we substitute $x = -2$ into the expression:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & <5x-3 \\\\ y & <5(-2)-3 \\\\ y & <-13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the values of $y$ that satisfy the system are $y < -13$."] [17592186208428 :question/worked-solution "We substitute $y = 10$ into each inequality to determine which $x$-values satisfy the system of inequalities.\n\n- Substituting $y = 10$ into the first inequality, we obtain\n$10 \\leq 12. \\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n- Substituting $y = 10$ into the second inequality $y > \\dfrac{x}{2} + 2$ and solving for $x$, we get:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10 & >\\frac{x}{2}+2 \\\\ 8 & >\\frac{x}{2} \\\\ x & <16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, among the answer choices, only $x = 15$ satisfies both inequalities."] [17592186208429 :question/worked-solution "We substitute each ordered pair into the inequalities to check which one satisfies both conditions.\n\n- For $(2, 3)$:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y>2x-1 &  & \\hspace{1em}\\Rightarrow \\hspace{1em}3>2(2)-1=3\\hspace{1em}{\\color{red}{\\times }} \\\\  & x+y<4 &  & \\hspace{1em}\\Rightarrow \\hspace{1em}2+3=5<4\\hspace{1em}{\\color{red}{\\times }}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- For $(1, 1)$:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y>2x-1 &  & \\hspace{1em}\\Rightarrow \\hspace{1em}1>2(1)-1=1\\hspace{1em}{\\color{red}{\\times }} \\\\  & x+y<4 &  & \\hspace{1em}\\Rightarrow \\hspace{1em}1+1=2<4\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}{✓}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- For $(2, 2)$:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y>2x-1 &  & \\hspace{1em}\\Rightarrow \\hspace{1em}2>2(2)-1=3\\hspace{1em}{\\color{red}{\\times }} \\\\  & x+y<4 &  & \\hspace{1em}\\Rightarrow \\hspace{1em}2+2=4<4\\hspace{1em}{\\color{red}{\\times }}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For $(1, 2)$:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y>2x-1 &  & \\hspace{1em}\\Rightarrow \\hspace{1em}2>2(1)-1=1\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}{✓}} \\\\  & x+y<4 &  & \\hspace{1em}\\Rightarrow \\hspace{1em}1+2=3<4\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}{✓}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only the pair $(1, 2)$ satisfies both inequalities."] [17592186208432 :question/worked-solution "We are told that, according to the triangle inequality theorem, the sum of any two sides of a triangle must be greater than the third side.\n\nApplying the triangle inequality to the three combinations of side lengths, we obtain the following three inequalities:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7+10 & >x \\\\ 7+x & >10 \\\\ 10+x & >7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we simplify each inequality:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & <17 \\\\ x & >3 \\\\ x & >-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, since side lengths are positive, the valid lower bound is $x > 3$. So, we have\n$3 < x < 17$.\n\nTherefore, the correct inequality is $3 < x < 17$."] [17592186208448 :question/worked-solution "In $\\triangle ABC$, the sum of the measures of the interior angles is always $180^\\circ$.\n\nThus, the sum of the measures of $\\angle A$ and $\\angle B$ must be *less than* $180^\\circ$, as there must be room for $\\angle C$. This means\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m\\mathrm{∠}A+m\\mathrm{∠}B & <{180}^{∘} \\\\ {67}^{∘}+m\\mathrm{∠}B & <{180}^{∘} \\\\ m\\mathrm{∠}B & <{113}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, among the choices, only $109^\\circ$ could be the measure of $\\angle B$."] [17592186208532 :question/worked-solution "We are asked to determine which table contains only points that satisfy the system of inequalities $v > u + 1$ and $u > 8$. To do this, we check that each point satisfies both conditions.\n\nThe correct table is the following:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$u$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$v$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$11$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNotice that for the points $(9, 11)$, $(10, 12)$, and $(12, 15)$, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}11 & >9+1=10\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}{✓}} \\\\ 12 & >10+1=11\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}{✓}} \\\\ 15 & >12+1=13\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}{✓}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9 & >8\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}{✓}} \\\\ 10 & >8\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}{✓}} \\\\ 12 & >8\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}{✓}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBoth inequalities are satisfied for all points."] [17592186209211 :question/problem "$$\n3|2-x|+2|2-x|=20\n$$\nThe positive solution of the equation given above is $x =$ {{field-1}}."] [17592186209351 :question/problem "$$\n3|4-a|-7|4-a|=-60\n$$\nThe positive solution of the equation given above is $a =$ {{field-1}}."] [17592186212995 :question/worked-solution "For our equation to have infinitely many solutions, both sides of our equation must be equivalent expressions. So, we must match our coefficients and constants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{5}{6}ax + (-4) &= \\frac{5}{2}x + (8b)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis yields the two equations\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{6}a & =\\frac{5}{2}\\hspace{2em} & \\text{and}\\hspace{2em}-4 & =8b \\\\ a & =\\frac{6}{5}\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}\\hspace{2em} & \\text{and}\\hspace{2em}b & =-\\frac{4}{8} \\\\ a & =3\\hspace{2em} & \\text{and}\\hspace{2em}b & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a}{b}=\\displaystyle{\\dfrac{3}{-1/2}=3\\cdot(-2)=-6}\n$$"] [17592186216808 :question/worked-solution "A quadratic equation has no real solutions if the discriminant is negative: $\\mathcal{D} < 0$.\n\nTo compute the discriminant, note the following coefficients: $a = k$, $b = 6$ and $c = k$.\n\nSo, we require\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathcal{D} & <0 \\\\ {b}^{2}-4ac & <0 \\\\ (6)^{2}-4(k)(k) & <0 \\\\ 36-4{k}^{2} & <0 \\\\ 36 & <4{k}^{2} \\\\ {k}^{2} & >9 \\\\ \\sqrt{{k}^{2}} & >\\sqrt{9} \\\\ |k| & >3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $\\mid k \\mid > 3$, then $k \\lt -3$ or $k \\gt 3$. Using interval notation, then, our solution is $k \\in (-\\infty, -3) \\cup (3, \\infty)$."] [17592186216810 :question/worked-solution "A quadratic equation has no real solutions if the discriminant is negative: $\\mathcal{D} < 0$.\n\nFirst, rewrite the equation in standard quadratic form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(px - 24) &= -9 \\\\\npx^{2} - 24x &= -9 \\\\\npx^{2} - 24x + 9 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficients of our quadratic equation are the following:\n\n$$\na = p, \\qquad b = -24, \\qquad c = 9\n$$\n\nSo, computing the discriminant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD &= b^{2} - 4ac \\\\\n&= (-24)^{2} - 4(p)(9) \\\\\n&= 576 - 36p\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo have no real solutions, we need $\\mathcal{D} < 0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}576-36p & <0 \\\\ -36p & <-576 \\\\ p & >\\frac{-576}{-36} \\\\ p & >16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $p$ must be an integer, we conclude that the least possible value is $17$."] [17592186216813 :question/worked-solution "The line $y = m$ intersects the parabola\n\n$$\ny = 4x^2 - 8x + 7\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n4x^2 - 8x + 7 = m\n$$\n\nA quadratic equation has two distinct real roots if the discriminant is positive: $\\mathcal{D} > 0$.\n\nFirst, rewrite the equation in standard quadratic form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x^{2} - 8x + 7 &= m \\\\\n4x^{2} - 8x + 7 - m &= 0 \\\\\n4x^{2} - 8x + (7 - m) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficients of our quadratic equation are the following:\n\n$$\na = 4, \\qquad b = -8, \\qquad c = 7 - m\n$$\n\nSo, computing the discriminant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD &= b^{2} - 4ac \\\\\n&= (-8)^{2} - 4(4)(7 - m) \\\\\n&= 64 - 112 + 16m \\\\\n&=-48 + 16m\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor two distinct real solutions, we need $\\mathcal{D} > 0{:}$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-48+16m & >0 \\\\ 16m & >48 \\\\ m & >3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $m$ must be an integer, we conclude that the least possible value is\n$4$."] [17592186216817 :question/worked-solution "The line $y=k$ intersects the parabola\n\n$$\ny = -3x^2 - 6x + 9\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n-3x^2 - 6x + 9 = k\n$$\n\nA quadratic equation has no real roots if the discriminant is negative: $\\mathcal{D} < 0$.\n\nFirst, rewrite the equation in standard quadratic form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-3x^{2} - 6x + 9 &= k \\\\\n- 3x^{2} - 6x + 9 - k &= 0 \\\\\n- 3x^{2} - 6x + (9 - k) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficients of our quadratic equation are the following:\n\n$$\na = -3, \\qquad b = -6, \\qquad c = 9-k\n$$\n\nSo, computing the discriminant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD &= b^{2} - 4ac \\\\\n&= (-6)^{2} - 4(-3)(9 - k) \\\\\n&= 36 + 12(9 - k) \\\\\n&= 36 + 108 - 12k \\\\\n&= 144 - 12k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor no real solutions, we need $\\mathcal{D} < 0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}144-12k & <0 \\\\ -12k & <-144 \\\\ 12k & >144 \\\\ k & >\\frac{144}{12} \\\\ k & >12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $k$ must be an integer, we conclude that the smallest possible value is $13$."] [17592186216822 :question/worked-solution "First, recall that the parabola is symmetric about the vertical line passing through the vertex. Because $g(3) = g(9)$, the vertex is midway between $x_1 = 3$ and $x_2 = 9$.\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinate of the vertex is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nh &= \\frac{x_{1} + x_{2}}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3 + 9}{2} \\\\\n&= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince at the vertex, $g(h) = k$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(6) &= a(6)^{2} - 12(6) + c \\\\\nk &= 36a - 72 + c\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the parabola has vertex below the $x$-axis, $k < 0$, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}36a-72+c & <0 \\\\ 36a+c & <72.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false because $c$ does not always have to be negative. For example, if the parabola opens upward with $a = 1 > 0$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}36(1)+c & <72 \\\\ c & <36,\\end{aligned}\n$$\nwhich could be positive.\n- Statement II is true. Since the parabola opens upward, we must have $a > 0$.\n- Statement III is false because $c$ does not always have to be positive. For example, if the parabola opens upward with $a = 3 > 0$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}36(3)+c & <72 \\\\ c & <-36,\\end{aligned}\n$$\nwhich is negative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."] [17592186216849 :question/worked-solution "The line $y=k-3x$ intersects the parabola\n\n$$\ny=3x^2 - 12x + 8\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n3x^2 - 12x + 8 = k - 3x\n$$\n\nA quadratic equation has two distinct real roots if the discriminant is positive: $\\mathcal{D} > 0$.\n\nFirst, rewrite the equation in standard quadratic form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x^{2} - 12x + 8 &= k - 3x \\\\\n3x^{2} - 12x + 8 + 3x - k &= 0 \\\\\n3x^{2} - 9x + (8 - k) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficients of our quadratic equation are the following:\n\n$$\na = 3, \\qquad b = -9, \\qquad c = 8-k\n$$\n\nSo, computing the discriminant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD &= b^{2} - 4ac \\\\\n&= (-9)^{2} - 4(3)(8 - k) \\\\\n&= 81 - 96 + 12k \\\\\n&=-15 + 12k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor two distinct real solutions, we need $\\mathcal{D} > 0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-15+12k & >0 \\\\ 12k & >15 \\\\ k & >\\frac{15}{12} \\\\ k & >1.25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $k$ must be an integer, we conclude that the least possible value is $2$."] [17592186216850 :question/worked-solution "The line\n\n$$\ny=x+k\n$$\n\nintersects the parabola\n\n$$\ny = 2x^2 - 4x + 8\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n2x^2 - 4x + 8 = x+k\n$$\n\nA quadratic equation has no real roots if the discriminant is negative: $\\mathcal{D} < 0$.\n\nFirst, rewrite the equation in standard quadratic form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x^{2} - 4x + 8 &= x + k \\\\\n2x^{2} - 4x + 8 - x - k &= 0 \\\\\n2x^{2} - 5x + (8 - k) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficients of our quadratic equation are the following:\n\n$$\na = 2, \\qquad b = -5, \\qquad c = 8-k\n$$\n\nSo, computing the discriminant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD &= b^{2} - 4ac \\\\\n&= (-5)^{2} - 4(2)(8 - k) \\\\\n&= 25 - 8(8 - k) \\\\\n&= 25 - 64 + 8k \\\\\n&= 8k - 39\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor no real solutions, we need $\\mathcal{D} < 0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8k-39 & <0 \\\\ 8k & <39 \\\\ k & <\\frac{39}{8} \\\\ k & <4.875\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $k$ must be an integer, we conclude that the greatest possible value is $4$."] [17592186216888 :question/worked-solution "To compute the discriminant, note the following coefficients: $a=2$, $b=-2k$ and $c=3k-4$.\n\nSo, we require\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathcal{D} & <0 \\\\ {b}^{2}-4ac & <0 \\\\ (-2k)^{2}-4(2)(3k-4) & <0 \\\\ 4{k}^{2}-8(3k-4) & <0 \\\\ 4{k}^{2}-24k+32 & <0 \\\\ {k}^{2}-6k+8 & <0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider the parabola\n\n$$\ny=k^2 - 6k + 8\n$$\n\nand find its solutions. We obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nk^{2} - 6k + 8 &= 0 \\\\\nk^{2} - 2k - 4k + 8 &= 0 \\\\\nk(k - 2) - 4(k - 2) &= 0 \\\\\n(k - 2)(k - 4) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the solutions are $k=2$ and $k=4$. Since the leading coefficient is positive, the parabola opens upward. Therefore, we get the following graph:\n\n![](images/32/32dd90a9598bbe7d98af1fb3f4c13c830c8de166b679e211c92ff8c073b92452.png)\n\nWe want\n\n$$\ny =k^2 - 6k + 8 < 0\n$$\n\nmeaning that the graph lies below the $k$-axis. Based on the graph, the corresponding interval is $(2,4)$.\n\nTherefore, the given equation has no real solutions when $k \\in (2, 4)$."] [17592186216918 :question/worked-solution "![](images/77/774314ec80771e07338c2217da497b8a83b1795356756188875f7070a5275cb5.png)\n\nThe perpendicular bisector of the chord must pass through the circle's center. Therefore, the perpendicular bisectors of two different chords of the same circle must intersect at the center.\n\nSo, to find the center of the circle, we need to find where the perpendicular bisectors $l_1$ and $l_2$ intersect.\n\nThe equations of the lines are\n\n$$\n\\begin{cases}\\begin{aligned}y & =-x \\\\ y & =2x+12.\\end{aligned}\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to each other, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-x &= 2x + 12 \\\\\n- 3x &= 12 \\\\\nx &= -4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=-4$ into the first equation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= -x = - (-4) = 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the circle's center is at $(-4,4)$."] [17592186216982 :question/worked-solution "Let's compute the mean absolute deviation for Team B's scores.\n\nSince we are given Team B's mean (${\\color{blue}5}$), we subtract it from each number in the data set, find the absolute value of the results, and add all these together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|7-{\\color{blue}{5}}|+|5-{\\color{blue}{5}}|+|8-{\\color{blue}{5}}|+|4-{\\color{blue}{5}}|+|1-{\\color{blue}{5}}| &= |\\hspace{0.167em}2\\hspace{0.167em}|+|\\hspace{0.167em}0\\hspace{0.167em}|+|\\hspace{0.167em}3\\hspace{0.167em}|+|-1|+|-4| \\\\ &= 2+0+3+1+4 \\\\ &= {\\color{red}{10}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the mean absolute deviation (MAD), we divide the sum obtained above (${\\color{red}10}$) by the total number of data points ($5$):\n\n$$\n\\text{MAD} = \\dfrac{\\color{red}10}{5} = 2\n$$\n\nSo, Team B's scores have a mean absolute deviation of $2$ home runs.\n\nLet's now examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false.\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Since $1.5 < 2$, Team $A$'s home run scores had a smaller spread than Team $B$'s.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""] [17592186218232 :tutorial/content "Recall that for a quadratic equation\n\n$$\nax^2 + bx + c = 0\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\na\\neq 0\n$$\n\nthe discriminant is defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{D} = b^2 - 4ac\n$$\n\nA quadratic equation has no real solutions if the discriminant is negative: $\\mathcal{D} < 0$.\n\nIf a quadratic equation contains an unknown parameter and we know the equation has no real solutions, we can use the condition $\\mathcal D < 0$ to place conditions on the unknown parameter.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's consider the equation\n\n$$\n2x^2-5kx+10 = 0\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a real parameter. We wish to find *all* the values of $k$ for which the quadratic equation has no real solutions.\n\nTo compute the discriminant, we first note that the coefficients are\n\n$$\na=2,\\quad b=-5k,\\quad c=10\n$$\n\nSo, we require\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathcal{D} & <0 \\\\ {b}^{2}-4ac & <0 \\\\ (-5k)^{2}-4(2)(10) & <0 \\\\ 25{k}^{2}-80 & <0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a quadratic inequality in the variable $k$. Solving this equation for $k$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25{k}^{2}-80 & <0 \\\\ 25{k}^{2} & <80 \\\\ {k}^{2} & <\\frac{16}{5} \\\\ \\sqrt{{k}^{2}} & <\\sqrt{\\frac{16}{5}} \\\\ |k| & <\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ |k| & <\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our solution is\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{4\\sqrt{5}}{5} < k < \\dfrac{4\\sqrt{5}}{5}\n$$\n\nWe can also express this solution using interval notation:\n\n$$\nk \\in \\left(-\\dfrac{4\\sqrt{5}}{5},\\dfrac{4\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\right)\n$$\n\nAny value of $k$ in this interval will give a quadratic equation with no real solutions."] [17592186218246 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we’ll learn how to use the discriminant to determine the values of unknown parameters that make a quadratic system have either two solutions (two intersection points) or no solutions (no intersection points).\n\nLet's consider the following quadratic system:\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = 4 {x}^{2} - 8 x + 5 \\\\ y = m\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nIn the system above, $m$ is an integer constant. Let's find the least possible value of $m$ such that the graphs of these functions intersect at two distinct points in the $xy$-plane.\n\nThe line $y=m$ intersects the parabola\n\n$$\ny=4x^2 - 8x + 5\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n4x^2 - 8x + 5 = m\n$$\n\nA quadratic equation has two distinct real roots if the discriminant is positive: $\\mathcal{D} > 0$.\n\nFirst, rewrite the equation in standard quadratic form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x^{2} - 8x + 5 &= m \\\\\n4x^{2} - 8x + 5 - m &= 0 \\\\\n4x^{2} - 8x + (5 - m) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficients of our quadratic equation are the following:\n\n$$\na = 4, \\qquad b = -8, \\qquad c = 5-m\n$$\n\nSo, computing the discriminant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD &= b^{2} - 4ac \\\\\n&= (-8)^{2} - 4(4)(5 - m) \\\\\n&= 64 - 80 + 16m \\\\\n&=-16 + 16m\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor two distinct real solutions, we need $\\mathcal{D} > 0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-16+16m & >0 \\\\ 16m & >16 \\\\ m & >1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $m$ must be an integer, we conclude that the least possible value is $2$."] [17592186218247 :tutorial/content "Now, let’s see an example where there are no intersection points.\n\nIf the graph of the equation\n\n$$\ny = x^2 - 4x + 7\n$$\n\ndoesn't intersect the line $y=k$ in the $xy$-plane, where $k$ is an integer constant, let's find the greatest possible value of $k$.\n\nThe line $y=k$ intersects the parabola\n\n$$\ny = x^2 - 4x + 7\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nx^2 - 4x + 7 = k\n$$\n\nA quadratic equation has no real roots if the discriminant is negative: $\\mathcal{D} < 0$.\n\nFirst, rewrite the equation in standard quadratic form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 4x + 7 &= k \\\\\nx^{2} - 4x + 7 - k &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2} - 4x + (7 - k) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficients of our quadratic equation are the following:\n\n$$\na = 1, \\qquad b = -4, \\qquad c = 7-k\n$$\n\nSo, computing the discriminant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD &= (-4)^{2} - 4(1)(7 - k) \\\\\n&= 16 - 4(7 - k) \\\\\n&= 16 - 28 + 4k \\\\\n&= 4k - 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor no real solutions, we need $\\mathcal{D} < 0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4k-12 & <0 \\\\ 4k & <12 \\\\ k & <\\frac{12}{4} \\\\ k & <3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $k$ must be an integer, we conclude that the greatest possible value is $2$."] [17592186218248 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we'll learn how to represent geometric properties of 2D shapes using algebraic functions.\nThis allows us to translate real-world measurements into mathematical (algebraic) models that we can analyze.\n\nWe'll begin by learning to interpret algebraic expressions representing geometric properties.\n\nFor example, suppose we are given that the area $A$, in square centimeters, of a rectangular solar panel can be represented by the expression\n\n$$\nA = w(w + 14)\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is the width, in centimeters, of the panel. What expression represents the length, in centimeters, of the panel in this expression?\n\nThe area of a rectangle is given by\n\n$$\nA = \\text{width} \\cdot \\text{length}\n$$\n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\nA = w \\cdot (w + 14)\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is the width.\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rccc}A\\hspace{1em}= & {\\color{blue}{\\text{width}}} & \\cdot  & {\\color{red}{\\text{length}}} \\\\  & ↕ &  & ↕ \\\\ A\\hspace{1em}= & {\\color{blue}{w}} & \\cdot  & {\\color{red}{(w+14)}}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nTherefore, comparing the two expressions, we conclude that the length must be\n\n$$\nw + 14\n$$"] [17592186218267 :tutorial/content "In previous lessons, we learned how to determine conditions on unknown parameters in quadratic equations to force a particular number of solutions. As we saw, this process often involves solving a quadratic inequality.\n\nIn this lesson, we'll consider cases where the quadratic inequality that arises from these problems must be solved using a graphical method.\n\nFor example, for which values of $k$ does the equation\n\n$$\n3x^2-6kx+k=0\n$$\n\nhave two distinct real solutions?\n\nRecall that a quadratic equation has two distinct real solutions if its discriminant is positive: $\\mathcal{D}>0$.\n\nTo compute the discriminant, we first note the equation's coefficients:\n\n$$\na=3,\\quad b=-6k,\\quad c=k\n$$\n\nSo, we require\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathcal{D} & >0 \\\\ {b}^{2}-4ac & >0 \\\\ (-6k)^{2}-4(3)(k) & >0 \\\\ 36{k}^{2}-12k & >0 \\\\ 3{k}^{2}-k & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider the parabola $y=3k^2-k$ and find its solutions. We obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3k^{2} - k &= 0 \\\\\nk(3k - 1) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the solutions are\n\n$$\nk=0\\quad \\text{and}\\quad k=\\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nSince the leading coefficient is positive, the parabola opens upward. Therefore, we get the following graph:\n\n![](images/4d/4deda3a2118d71d65e47b9ee7d2419df2f0c258652b650a3e7288879cdedd170.png)\n\nWe want\n\n$$\ny = 3k^2 - k > 0\n$$\n\nmeaning that the graph lies above the $k$-axis. Based on the graph, the corresponding intervals are $(-\\infty,0)$ or\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{1}{3},\\infty\\right)\n$$\n\nTherefore, our equation has two distinct real solutions when\n\n$$\nk \\in (-\\infty,0) \\cup \\left(\\dfrac{1}{3},\\infty\\right)\n$$"] [17592186218277 :tutorial/content "Let's now consider cases where we transform higher-order polynomials.\n\nSuppose we have the function\n\n$$\nf(x) = (x+4)(x-6)^2\n$$\n\nand we are told that\n\n$$\nf(12-k) = 0\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant. What are the possible values of $k$?\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is written in factored form, so we can immediately deduce its zeros:\n\n- From the factor $(x+4)$, we get $x=-4$.\n- From the factor $(x-6)^2$, we get $x=6$.\n\nNotice that $x=6$ is a *double root* because the factor $(x-6)$ is squared. So the zeros of $f$ are $x=-4$ and $x=6$.\n\nThe equation\n\n$$\nf(12-k)=0\n$$\n\nmeans that the input $12-k$ must be equal to one of the zeros of $f$.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12-k=-4\\hspace{1em} & \\Rightarrow \\hspace{1em}k=16, \\\\ 12-k=6\\hspace{1em} & \\Rightarrow \\hspace{1em}k=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the possible values of $k$ are $6$ and $16$."] [17592186220990 :question/worked-solution "To find the fewest number of classes that makes the unlimited pass worthwhile, we assume each class costs the highest price of $\\$9.50$.\n\nLet $x$ represent the number of classes attended.\n\nWithout the pass, the total cost is $\\$9.50$ per class:\n$9.50x$\n\nWith the pass, the cost is a flat $\\$135$.\n\nWe want the pass to cost less than paying individually. This gives the inequality:\n\n$$\n135 < 9.50x\n$$\n\nNow, solve the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}135 & <9.50x \\\\ \\frac{135}{9.50} & <x \\\\ 14.210… & <x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x$ must be a whole number, the smallest value that satisfies the inequality is $15$."] [17592186221036 :question/worked-solution "To find the slope of the line $4x - y = 1$, we rewrite the equation in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n-y = -4x + 1 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad y = 4x - 1\n$$\n\nSo, the slope is $4$.\n\nNow, a line is perpendicular to another line when its slope is the negative reciprocal of that other line's slope. That means the second line must have slope\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nFrom the line equation\n\n$$\nmx + ny = 1\n$$\n\nit follows that the slope of the second line is\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{m}{n}\n$$\n\nSo we set\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{m}{n} = -\\dfrac{1}{4} \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\dfrac{m}{n} = \\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nNow, let's utilise this information to check which pair of lines is perpendicular:\n\n- First, we consider the following lines:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+y=1\\hspace{2em} & \\Rightarrow \\hspace{2em}y=-4x+1 \\\\ mx+2ny=1\\hspace{2em} & \\Rightarrow \\hspace{2em}y=-\\frac{m}{2n}x+\\frac{1}{2n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThe slope of the first line is $-4$.\nThe slope of the second line is\n$$\n\\displaystyle -\\frac{m}{2n}|=-\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{m}{n}; =-\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{4}; =-\\frac{1}{8}.\n$$\nSince the product of slopes does not equal $-1$, this pair of lines is NOT perpendicular.\n- Next, we consider the following lines:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+y=1\\hspace{2em} & \\Rightarrow \\hspace{2em}y=-4x+1 \\\\ mx-ny=1\\hspace{2em} & \\Rightarrow \\hspace{2em}y=\\frac{m}{n}x-\\frac{1}{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThe slope of the first line is $-4$.\nThe slope of the second line is\n$\\dfrac m n = \\dfrac 1 4$.\nSince the product of the slopes equals $-1$, this pair of lines is perpendicular.\n- Finally, we consider the following lines:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8x-2y=1\\hspace{2em} & \\Rightarrow \\hspace{2em}y=4x-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 2mx+ny=1\\hspace{2em} & \\Rightarrow \\hspace{2em}y=-\\frac{2m}{n}x+\\frac{1}{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThe slope of the first line is $4$.\nThe slope of the second line is\n$$\n\\displaystyle -\\frac{2m}{n}|=-2 \\cdot \\frac{m}{n}; =-2 \\cdot \\frac{1}{4}; =-\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\nSince the product of slopes does not equal $-1$, this pair of lines is NOT perpendicular.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""] [17592186221068 :question/worked-solution "To determine how many solutions a system of linear equations has, we check whether the two equations represent:\n\n- The same line, which implies there are infinitely many solutions;\n- Parallel lines that never intersect, which implies there is no solution;\n- Two distinct lines that intersect once, which implies there is exactly one solution.\n\nLet’s analyze the system:\n\n$$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-5x - 15y &=25 \\\\ - x - 3y &=5\\end{aligned}\\right.$$\n\nNote that if we multiply both sides of the second equation by $5$, we get exactly the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5(-x - 3y) &= 5(5) \\\\\n- 5x - 15y &= 25\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the two equations represent the same line, and any point that lies on one will also lie on the other.\n\nTherefore, the system has infinitely many solutions."] [17592186221072 :question/worked-solution "Since lines $p$ and $q$ are parallel, and the line $t$ acts as a transversal, the angle labeled $a^\\circ$ and the angle adjacent to $b^\\circ$ outside the parallel lines are corresponding angles. Hence, they are congruent.\n\n![](images/ed/edbb26b99c0bafe9a3281749e2d70045c46c7e916bfba5066b9f5f57eaaadbfb.png)\n\nThe angle labeled $a^\\circ$ and the adjacent angle form a linear pair, creating a straight angle at the point of intersection of $t$ and $q$. The measure of a straight angle is $180^\\circ$, so we can write\n\n$$\nb + a = 180\n$$\n\nSo, statement II is false.\n\nNext, we are told that $a > 105$. Substituting this into the equation, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & >105 \\\\ b+a & >b+105 \\\\ 180 & >b+105 \\\\ 75 & >b \\\\ b & <75.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, statement I is true while statement III is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."] [17592186221081 :question/worked-solution "We simplify the inequality by dividing *all terms* by the greatest common factor of $5{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{25p+10q}{5} & >\\frac{15}{5} \\\\ 5p+2q & >3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the inequality $25p + 10q > 15$ is equivalent to\n$5p + 2q > 3$."] [17592186221087 :question/worked-solution "Let $s$ represent the number of $25$-book boxes and $t$ represent the number of $60$-book boxes.\n\nWe are given two constraints:\n\n- The total number of boxes must be at most $20{:}$\n$s + t \\leq 20$\n- The total number of books must be at least $500{:}$\n$25s + 60t \\geq 500$\n\nWe want to *maximize* the number of $25$-book boxes, $s$, subject to these constraints.\n\nNow, we assume $t = 0$ to check the highest possible $s$ that satisfies both conditions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25s+60t & \\ge 500 \\\\ 25s+60(0) & \\ge 500 \\\\ 25s & \\ge 500 \\\\ s & \\ge \\frac{500}{25}=20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the largest possible value is $s = 20$.\n\nWe check if this value satisfies the total number of books condition:\n\n$$\ns + t=20 + 0=20\\leq 20 \\quad{\\color{green}\\checkmark}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum number of $25$-book boxes is $20$."] [17592186221943 :question/problem "Given that\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathcal{L}\\{\\operatorname{sin}\\omega t\\} & =\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0, \\\\ \\mathcal{L}\\{\\operatorname{cos}\\omega t\\} & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\nfind $L{u(t - 4)\\sin (10πt)}$, where $u(t)$ is the unit step function."] [17592186222025 :question/problem "$$\nF(x)=\\begin{cases}{x}^{2}, & x\\in [0,3) \\\\ -3x, & x\\in [-3,0)\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nThe above function $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [-3,3).$ Let $f(x)$ be the $6$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R.$\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"right\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">I.</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"left\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$F(x)$ is continuous on $x\\in (-3,3)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">{{field-1}}</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"right\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">II.</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"left\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-3,3)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">{{field-2}}</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"right\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">III.</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"left\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\lim_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}F(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}F(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">{{field-3}}</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"right\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">IV.</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"left\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$f(x)$ is piecewise smooth and continuous on $R$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">{{field-4}}</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [17592186222026 :question/problem "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & x\\in [0,1) \\\\ x, & x\\in [-1,0)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above function $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [-1,1).$ \nSelect the correct options below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"right\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">I.</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"left\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$F(x)$ is continuous on $x\\in (-1,1)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">{{field-1}}</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"right\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">II.</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"left\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-1,1)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">{{field-2}}</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"right\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">III.</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"left\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\lim_{x\\to -{1}^{+}}F(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}F(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">{{field-3}}</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"right\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">IV.</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"left\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$f(x)$ is piecewise smooth and continuous on $R$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">{{field-4}}</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [17592186224846 :question/worked-solution "Let's solve the first two equations:\n\n- To factor the left-hand side of $m^2 + 9m + 14 = 0$, we need to find two numbers that multiply to $14$ and add to $9$. These two numbers are $7$ and $2$, so we can factor the left-hand side as follows:\n$$\n\\displaystyle m^{2} + 9m + 14|= 0; m^{2}\\underbrace{+ 7m + 2m}_{9m} + 14|= 0; m(m + 7) + 2(m + 7)|= 0; (m + 7)(m + 2)|= 0\n$$\nApplying the zero product rule, we have the following:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  & m+7=0 & \\hspace{2em}\\Rightarrow \\hspace{2em}m=-7 \\\\  &  & m+2=0 & \\hspace{2em}\\Rightarrow \\hspace{2em}m=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThus, the solutions $r$ and $s$, with $r < s$, are the following:\n$$\n\\displaystyle r =-7ands =-2\n$$\n- To factor the left-hand side of $m^2 + 5m + 4 = 0$, we need to find two numbers that multiply to $4$ and add to $5$. These two numbers are $1$ and $4$, so we can factor the left-hand side as follows:\n$$\n\\displaystyle m^{2} + 5m + 4|= 0; m^{2}\\underbrace{+ m + 4m}_{5m} + 4|= 0; m(m + 1) + 4(m + 1)|= 0; (m + 1)(m + 4)|= 0\n$$\nApplying the zero product rule, we have the following:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  & m+1=0 & \\hspace{2em}\\Rightarrow \\hspace{2em}m=-1 \\\\  &  & m+4=0 & \\hspace{2em}\\Rightarrow \\hspace{2em}m=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThus, the solutions $t$ and $u$, with $t < u$, are the following:\n$$\n\\displaystyle t =-4andu =-1\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr + t &= -7 + (-4) =-11, \\\\\ns + u &= -2 + (-1) =-3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we are given that both $r+t$ and $s+u$ are solutions to the equation\n\n$$\nm^2 + 14m + k = 0\n$$\n\nSo, substituting\n\n$$\nm = s+u = -3\n$$\n\ninto this equation (it also works to substitute\n\n$$\nm=r+t=-11\n$$\n\n) and solving for $k$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(-3)^{2} + 14(-3) + k &= 0 \\\\\n9 - 42 + k &= 0 \\\\\n- 33 + k &= 0 \\\\\nk &= 33\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186224903 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we may factor the system of equations as follows:\n\n$$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=3x - 5 \\\\ y &=-2x^{2} + 3x + 3\\end{aligned}\\right.$$\n\nSince both equations are solved for $y$, we set them equal and solve for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x - 5 &= -2x^{2} + 3x + 3 \\\\\n0 &= -2x^{2} + 8 \\\\\nx^{2} &= 4 \\\\\nx &= ± 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the zero product rule, we have\n\n$$\nx=-2\\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad x=2\n$$\n\nSince $-2<2$, the smallest possible value of $x$ is $-2$."] [17592186224904 :question/worked-solution "First, we evaluate $f(5){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(5) &= \\mid 5 - 4(5) \\mid \\\\\n&= \\mid 5 - 20 \\mid \\\\\n&= \\mid - 15 \\mid \\\\\n&= 15\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $f(5)$ into the equation\n\n$$\nf(5) - f(a) = -15\n$$\n\nand solve it for $f(a)$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(5) - f(a) &= -15 \\\\\n15 - f(a) &= -15 \\\\\n- f(a) &= -30 \\\\\nf(a) &= 30\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substitute\n\n$$\nf(a)= \\mid a - 4a \\mid\n$$\n\ninto the above and solve for $a{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(a) & =30 \\\\ |a-4a| & =30 \\\\ |-3a| & =30 \\\\ 3|a| & =30 \\\\ |a| & =10 \\\\ a & =\\pm 10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a$ could be $10$ or $-10$."] [17592186224993 :question/worked-solution "To factor the left-hand side, we need to find two numbers that multiply to $-30$ and add to $-1$. These two numbers are $-6$ and $5$, so we can factor the left-hand side as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nt^{2} - t - 30 &= 0 \\\\\nt^{2}\\underbrace{-6t + 5t}_{-t} - 30 &= 0 \\\\\nt(t - 6) + 5(t - 6) &= 0 \\\\\n(t - 6)(t + 5) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the zero product rule, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  & t-6=0 & \\hspace{2em}\\Rightarrow \\hspace{2em}t=6 \\\\  &  & t+5=0 & \\hspace{2em}\\Rightarrow \\hspace{2em}t=-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we are given that $k$ is a solution to our equation, with $k \\gt 0$. Therefore, we conclude that $k = 6$."] [17592186224994 :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite our equation in standard form and with a positive leading coefficient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-p^{2} - 3p + 4 &= 0 \\\\\np^{2} + 3p - 4 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo factor the left-hand side, we need to find two numbers that multiply to $-4$ and add to $3$. These two numbers are $4$ and $-1$, so we can factor the left-hand side as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np^{2} + 3p - 4 &= 0 \\\\\np^{2}\\underbrace{+ 4p - p}_{3p} - 4 &= 0 \\\\\np(p + 4) - 1(p + 4) &= 0 \\\\\n(p + 4)(p - 1) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the zero product rule, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  & p+4=0 & \\hspace{2em}\\Rightarrow \\hspace{2em}p=-4 \\\\  &  & p-1=0 & \\hspace{2em}\\Rightarrow \\hspace{2em}p=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the **positive** solution to the given equation is $p = 1$."] [17592186224995 :question/problem "What is the positive solution to the equation $4y^2 - 17y - 15 = 0$?\n\nExplanation:\n\nTo factor the left-hand side, we need two numbers that multiply to $4 \\cdot (-15) = -60$ and add to $-17$. These two numbers are $-20$ and $3$, so we can factor the left-hand side as follows:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle 4y^{2} - 17y - 15|= 0; 4y^{2}\\underbrace{-20y + 3y}_{-17y} - 15|= 0; 4y(y - 5) + 3(y - 5)|= 0; (y - 5)(4y + 3)|= 0\n$$\n\nApplying the zero product rule, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  & y-5=0 & \\hspace{2em}\\Rightarrow \\hspace{2em}y=5 \\\\  &  & 4y+3=0 & \\hspace{2em}\\Rightarrow \\hspace{2em}y=-\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the **positive** solution to the given equation is $y = 5$."] [17592186224995 :question/worked-solution "To factor the left-hand side, we need two numbers that multiply to\n\n$$\n4 \\cdot (-15) = -60\n$$\n\nand add to $-17$. These two numbers are $-20$ and $3$, so we can factor the left-hand side as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4y^{2} - 17y - 15 &= 0 \\\\\n4y^{2}\\underbrace{-20y + 3y}_{-17y} - 15 &= 0 \\\\\n4y(y - 5) + 3(y - 5) &= 0 \\\\\n(y - 5)(4y + 3) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the zero product rule, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  & y-5=0 & \\hspace{2em}\\Rightarrow \\hspace{2em}y=5 \\\\  &  & 4y+3=0 & \\hspace{2em}\\Rightarrow \\hspace{2em}y=-\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the **positive** solution to the given equation is $y = 5$."] [17592186225004 :question/worked-solution "A quadratic equation has no real roots if the discriminant is negative: $\\mathcal{D} < 0$.\n\nThe coefficients of our quadratic equation are the following:\n\n$$\na = 2, \\qquad b = q, \\qquad c = 50\n$$\n\nSo, computing the discriminant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD &= b^{2} - 4ac \\\\\n&= (q)^{2} - 4(2)(50) \\\\\n&= q^{2} - 400\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo have no real solutions, we need $\\mathcal{D} < 0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}^{2}-400 & <0 \\\\ {q}^{2} & <400 \\\\ \\sqrt{{q}^{2}} & <\\sqrt{400} \\\\ |q| & <20 \\\\ -20<q & <20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $q$ must be an integer, we conclude that the least possible value is $-19$."] [17592186225006 :question/worked-solution "A quadratic equation has no real solutions if the discriminant is negative: $\\mathcal{D} < 0$.\n\nFirst, rewrite the equation in standard quadratic form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(px - 24) &= -9 \\\\\npx^{2} - 24x &= -9 \\\\\npx^{2} - 24x + 9 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficients of our quadratic equation are the following:\n\n$$\na = p, \\qquad b = -24, \\qquad c = 9\n$$\n\nSo, computing the discriminant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD &= b^{2} - 4ac \\\\\n&= (-24)^{2} - 4(p)(9) \\\\\n&= 576 - 36p\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo have no real solutions, we need $\\mathcal{D} < 0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}576-36p & <0 \\\\ -36p & <-576 \\\\ p & >\\frac{-576}{-36} \\\\ p & >16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $p$ must be an integer, we conclude that the least possible value is $17$."] [17592186225007 :question/worked-solution "For a quadratic equation\n\n$$\nay^2 + by + c = 0\n$$\n\nthe discriminant is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{D} = b^2 - 4ac\n$$\n\n- If $\\mathcal{D} > 0$, there are two distinct real solutions.\n- If $\\mathcal{D} = 0$, there is one real solution.\n- If $\\mathcal{D} < 0$, there are no real solutions (the solutions are complex).\n\nFirst, rewrite the given equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n3y^{2} + 6y - k = 0\n$$\n\nWe have $a = 3$, $b = 6$, and $c = -k$.\n\nComputing the discriminant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD &= b^{2} - 4ac \\\\\n&= 6^{2} - 4(3)(-k) \\\\\n&= 36 + 12k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor no real solutions, we require $\\mathcal{D} < 0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}36+12k & <0 \\\\ 12k & <-36 \\\\ k & <-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, any $k$ less than $-3$ would work.\nNotice that among the given options, only $-4$ is less than $-3$.\n\nTherefore, $k = -4$ is the correct answer."] [17592186225009 :question/worked-solution "A quadratic equation has two distinct real solutions if the discriminant is positive: $\\mathcal{D} > 0$.\n\nFirst, rewrite the equation in standard quadratic form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(2x + 12) &= -k \\\\\n2x^{2} + 12x &= -k \\\\\n2x^{2} + 12x + k &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficients of our quadratic equation are the following:\n\n$$\na = 2, \\qquad b = 12, \\qquad c = k\n$$\n\nSo, computing the discriminant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD &= b^{2} - 4ac \\\\\n&= (12)^{2} - 4(2)(k) \\\\\n&= 144 - 8k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo have distinct real solutions, we need $\\mathcal{D} > 0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}144-8k & >0 \\\\ 144 & >8k \\\\ 8k & <144 \\\\ k & <\\frac{144}{8} \\\\ k & <18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $k$ must be an integer, we conclude that the greatest possible value is $17$."] [17592186225010 :question/worked-solution "For a quadratic equation\n\n$$\nat^2 + bt + c = 0\n$$\n\nthe discriminant is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{D} = b^2 - 4ac\n$$\n\n- If $\\mathcal{D} > 0$, there are two distinct real solutions.\n- If $\\mathcal{D} = 0$, there is exactly one real solution.\n- If $\\mathcal{D} < 0$, there are no real solutions (the solutions are complex).\n\nThe coefficients of our quadratic equation are the following:\n\n$$\na = 4, \\qquad b = k, \\qquad c = 36\n$$\n\nComputing the discriminant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD &= b^{2} - 4ac \\\\\n&= k^{2} - 4(4)(36) \\\\\n&= k^{2} - 576\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo have more than one real solution, we need $\\mathcal{D} > 0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{k}^{2}-576 & >0 \\\\ {k}^{2} & >576 \\\\ \\sqrt{{k}^{2}} & >\\sqrt{576} \\\\ |k| & >24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that among the given options, only $-26$ satisfies this condition.\n\nTherefore, $k = -26$ is the correct answer."] [17592186225017 :question/worked-solution "We are given the following system:\n\n$$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=9 \\\\ y &=4 \\cdot 2^{x - 2} + 1\\end{aligned}\\right.$$\n\nSubstituting $x = 9$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= 4 \\cdot 2^{7} + 1 \\\\\n&= 4 \\cdot 128 + 1 \\\\\n&= 512 + 1 \\\\\n&= 513\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that $y=513$."] [17592186225041 :question/worked-solution "First, recall that the parabola is symmetric about the vertical line passing through the vertex. Because $g(3) = g(9)$, the vertex is midway between $x_1 = 3$ and $x_2 = 9$.\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinate of the vertex is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nh &= \\frac{x_{1} + x_{2}}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3 + 9}{2} \\\\\n&= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince at the vertex, $g(h) = k$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(6) &= a(6)^{2} - 12(6) + c \\\\\nk &= 36a - 72 + c\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the parabola has vertex below the $x$-axis, $k < 0$, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}36a-72+c & <0 \\\\ 36a+c & <72.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false because $c$ does not always have to be negative. For example, if the parabola opens upward with $a = 1 > 0$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}36(1)+c & <72 \\\\ c & <36,\\end{aligned}\n$$\nwhich could be positive.\n- Statement II is true. Since the parabola opens upward, we must have $a > 0$.\n- Statement III is false because $c$ does not always have to be positive. For example, if the parabola opens upward with $a = 3 > 0$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}36(3)+c & <72 \\\\ c & <-36,\\end{aligned}\n$$\nwhich is negative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."] [17592186225058 :question/worked-solution "The line $y = m$ intersects the parabola\n\n$$\ny = 4x^2 - 8x + 7\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n4x^2 - 8x + 7 = m\n$$\n\nA quadratic equation has two distinct real roots if the discriminant is positive: $\\mathcal{D} > 0$.\n\nFirst, rewrite the equation in standard quadratic form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x^{2} - 8x + 7 &= m \\\\\n4x^{2} - 8x + 7 - m &= 0 \\\\\n4x^{2} - 8x + (7 - m) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficients of our quadratic equation are the following:\n\n$$\na = 4, \\qquad b = -8, \\qquad c = 7 - m\n$$\n\nSo, computing the discriminant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD &= b^{2} - 4ac \\\\\n&= (-8)^{2} - 4(4)(7 - m) \\\\\n&= 64 - 112 + 16m \\\\\n&=-48 + 16m\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor two distinct real solutions, we need $\\mathcal{D} > 0{:}$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-48+16m & >0 \\\\ 16m & >48 \\\\ m & >3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $m$ must be an integer, we conclude that the least possible value is\n$4$."] [17592186225059 :question/worked-solution "The line $y=k-3x$ intersects the parabola\n\n$$\ny=3x^2 - 12x + 8\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n3x^2 - 12x + 8 = k - 3x\n$$\n\nA quadratic equation has two distinct real roots if the discriminant is positive: $\\mathcal{D} > 0$.\n\nFirst, rewrite the equation in standard quadratic form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x^{2} - 12x + 8 &= k - 3x \\\\\n3x^{2} - 12x + 8 + 3x - k &= 0 \\\\\n3x^{2} - 9x + (8 - k) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficients of our quadratic equation are the following:\n\n$$\na = 3, \\qquad b = -9, \\qquad c = 8-k\n$$\n\nSo, computing the discriminant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD &= b^{2} - 4ac \\\\\n&= (-9)^{2} - 4(3)(8 - k) \\\\\n&= 81 - 96 + 12k \\\\\n&=-15 + 12k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor two distinct real solutions, we need $\\mathcal{D} > 0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-15+12k & >0 \\\\ 12k & >15 \\\\ k & >\\frac{15}{12} \\\\ k & >1.25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $k$ must be an integer, we conclude that the least possible value is $2$."] [17592186225060 :question/worked-solution "The line $y=k$ intersects the parabola\n\n$$\ny = -3x^2 - 6x + 9\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n-3x^2 - 6x + 9 = k\n$$\n\nA quadratic equation has no real roots if the discriminant is negative: $\\mathcal{D} < 0$.\n\nFirst, rewrite the equation in standard quadratic form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-3x^{2} - 6x + 9 &= k \\\\\n- 3x^{2} - 6x + 9 - k &= 0 \\\\\n- 3x^{2} - 6x + (9 - k) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficients of our quadratic equation are the following:\n\n$$\na = -3, \\qquad b = -6, \\qquad c = 9-k\n$$\n\nSo, computing the discriminant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD &= b^{2} - 4ac \\\\\n&= (-6)^{2} - 4(-3)(9 - k) \\\\\n&= 36 + 12(9 - k) \\\\\n&= 36 + 108 - 12k \\\\\n&= 144 - 12k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor no real solutions, we need $\\mathcal{D} < 0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}144-12k & <0 \\\\ -12k & <-144 \\\\ 12k & >144 \\\\ k & >\\frac{144}{12} \\\\ k & >12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $k$ must be an integer, we conclude that the smallest possible value is $13$."] [17592186225061 :question/worked-solution "The line\n\n$$\ny=x+k\n$$\n\nintersects the parabola\n\n$$\ny = 2x^2 - 4x + 8\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n2x^2 - 4x + 8 = x+k\n$$\n\nA quadratic equation has no real roots if the discriminant is negative: $\\mathcal{D} < 0$.\n\nFirst, rewrite the equation in standard quadratic form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x^{2} - 4x + 8 &= x + k \\\\\n2x^{2} - 4x + 8 - x - k &= 0 \\\\\n2x^{2} - 5x + (8 - k) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficients of our quadratic equation are the following:\n\n$$\na = 2, \\qquad b = -5, \\qquad c = 8-k\n$$\n\nSo, computing the discriminant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD &= b^{2} - 4ac \\\\\n&= (-5)^{2} - 4(2)(8 - k) \\\\\n&= 25 - 8(8 - k) \\\\\n&= 25 - 64 + 8k \\\\\n&= 8k - 39\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor no real solutions, we need $\\mathcal{D} < 0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8k-39 & <0 \\\\ 8k & <39 \\\\ k & <\\frac{39}{8} \\\\ k & <4.875\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $k$ must be an integer, we conclude that the greatest possible value is $4$."] [17592186225062 :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite our equation in standard form and with a positive leading coefficient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-12p^{2} &= -17p - 5 \\\\\n- 12p^{2} + 17p + 5 &= 0 \\\\\n12p^{2} - 17p - 5 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo factor the left-hand side, we need two numbers that multiply to\n\n$$\n12 \\cdot (-5) = -60\n$$\n\nand add to $-17$. These two numbers are $-20$ and $3$, so we can factor the left-hand side as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n12p^{2} - 17p - 5 &= 0 \\\\\n12p^{2}\\underbrace{-20p + 3p}_{-17p} - 5 &= 0 \\\\\n4p(3p - 5) + 1(3p - 5) &= 0 \\\\\n(4p + 1)(3p - 5) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the zero product rule, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  & 4p+1=0 & \\hspace{2em}\\Rightarrow \\hspace{2em}p=-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  &  & 3p-5=0 & \\hspace{2em}\\Rightarrow \\hspace{2em}p=\\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the **positive** solution to the given equation is\n\n$$\np = \\dfrac{5}{3}\n$$"] [17592186225073 :question/worked-solution "We begin by evaluating $c(-2){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nc(-2) &= 8 + \\mid 2(-2) + (-2)^{2} \\mid \\\\\n&= 8 + \\mid - 4 + 4 \\mid \\\\\n&= 8 + \\mid 0 \\mid \\\\\n&= 8 + 0 \\\\\n&= 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $c = 8$. Now, we evaluate\n\n$$\nd(c) = d(8){:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d(8) & =|\\frac{8-5}{2(8)}|+8+2 \\\\  & =|\\frac{3}{16}|+10 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{16}+10 \\\\  & =\\frac{3+160}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{163}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186225077 :question/worked-solution "To find the $x$-intercepts of the graph, we first set\n\n$$\nf(x) = 0{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x + 9)(x + 1) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is satisfied when either factor is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+9=0\\hspace{1em} & \\Rightarrow \\hspace{1em}x=-9 \\\\  & \\hspace{0.222em}\\text{or} \\\\ x+1=0\\hspace{1em} & \\Rightarrow \\hspace{1em}x=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-coordinates of the $x$-intercepts of the graph of $y = f(x)$ are:\n\n$$\nx = \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{-9} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad x = \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{-1}\n$$"] [17592186225085 :question/worked-solution "We are told that $f$ grows exponentially over time, so the relationship between $x$ and $f$ must have the form\n\n$$\nf(x) = a \\cdot b^x\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the initial amount and $b$ is the growth factor.\n\nFrom the table, we see that the value at time $x = 0$ is $1.80$, so\n\n$$\nf(0)=a = 1.80\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function has the form\n\n$$\nf(x) = 1.80 \\cdot b^{x}\n$$\n\nTo find the growth factor $b$, we use the value at $x = 2{:}$\n\n$$\nf(2)= 1.80\\cdot b^{2} = 1.91\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $b{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}^{2} & =\\frac{1.91}{1.80} \\\\ {b}^{2} & \\approx 1.061\\hspace{0.167em}11 \\\\ b & =\\sqrt{1.061\\hspace{0.167em}11} \\\\ b & \\approx 1.03\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the exponential equation is\n\n$$\nf(x) = 1.80 \\cdot (1.03)^x\n$$\n\nLet’s confirm that this equation works for $x = 4{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(4) &= 1.80 \\cdot (1.03)^{4} \\\\\n&\\approx 2.03\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich matches the value in the table.\n\nSo, the equation is\n\n$$\nf(x) = 1.80 \\cdot (1.03)^x\n$$"] [17592186225591 :question/problem "The functions $s$ and $t$ are defined by the given equations.\n$$\ns(u)=5+|{u}^{2}-u|,\\hspace{2em}t(p)=|\\frac{2p}{p+4}|+p-1\n$$\nIf $s(1) = c$, where $c$ is a constant, what is the value of $t(c)$?$t(c) =$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186225592 :question/problem "The functions $c$ and $d$ are defined by the given equations.\n$$\nc(m)=8+|2m+{m}^{2}|,\\hspace{2em}d(z)=|\\frac{z-5}{2z}|+z+2\n$$\nIf $c(-2) = c$, where $c$ is a constant, what is the value of $d(c)$?$d(c) =$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186228830 :question/worked-solution "At $x = 1$, the value of $7x+3$ is greater than the value of $7^x$. Indeed,\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7(1)+3 & \\overset{?}{>}{7}^{1} \\\\ 7+3 & \\overset{?}{>}7 \\\\ 10 & >7.\\hspace{2em}{\\color{green}{✓}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, at $x = 3$, the value of $7^x$ is greater than the value of $7x+3$. Indeed,\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{7}^{3} & \\overset{?}{>}7(3)+3 \\\\ 343 & \\overset{?}{>}21+3 \\\\ 343 & >24.\\hspace{2em}{\\color{green}{✓}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there is a value $c$ between $1$ and $3$ such that if $0 < x < c$, we have $7x+3 > 7^x$, and if $x > c$, we have $7^x > 7x+3$.\n\nTherefore, the correct statement is \"III only\"."] [17592186228943 :question/worked-solution "We apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for the piecewise function \n$$\nf(t) \n= {\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}{e}^{2 t} , \\hspace{1em} & t \\le  3 \\\\ 1 \\hspace{1em} & t > 3\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{e}^{2t}\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }1\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{e}^{2t-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{e}^{(2-s)t}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the improper integral diverges if $s \\leq 0.$\n\nFor $s > 0,$ we calculate our Laplace transform as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{e}^{(2-s)t}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2-s}{[{e}^{(2-s)t}]}_{0}^{3}+{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3(2-s)}-1}{2-s}+\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{3}^{a}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3(2-s)}-1}{2-s}+\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{-{e}^{-st}}{s}\\right)|}_{3}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3(2-s)}-1}{2-s}-\\frac{1}{s}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }({e}^{-as}-{e}^{-3s}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3(2-s)}-1}{2-s}-\\frac{1}{s}\\cdot (0-{e}^{-3s}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3(2-s)}-1}{2-s}+\\frac{{e}^{-3s}}{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nvalid for $s > 0.$"] [17592186228944 :question/problem "Which of the following functions are piecewise continuous on $x\\in [0,\\infty).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\begin{cases}1,\\hspace{0.222em} & ⌈x⌉\\text{ is even} \\\\ -1,\\hspace{0.222em} & ⌈x⌉\\text{ is odd}\\end{cases} \\\\ g(x) & =u(x-1)+u\\hspace{-0.167em}(x-\\frac{1}{2})+u\\hspace{-0.167em}(x-\\frac{1}{3})+⋯ \\\\ h(x) & =⌈\\frac{x}{2}⌉\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $u(x)$ is the unit step function, and $\\lceil x \\rceil$ is the ceiling function."] [17592186228955 :question/worked-solution "We apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the unit step function is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t)=\\begin{cases}0,\\hspace{1em} & t<0 \\\\ 1,\\hspace{1em} & t\\ge 0.\\end{cases}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for the piecewise functions $u(t-2)$ and $u(t-6),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t-2) & =\\begin{cases}0,\\hspace{1em} & t<2 \\\\ 1,\\hspace{1em} & t\\ge 2\\end{cases} \\\\ u(t-6) & =\\begin{cases}0,\\hspace{1em} & t<6 \\\\ 1,\\hspace{1em} & t\\ge 6.\\end{cases}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nu(t-2) + u(t - 6) = \n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}0 , \\hspace{1em} & t < 2 \\\\ 1 , \\hspace{1em} & 2 \\le  t < 6 \\\\ 2 , \\hspace{1em} & t \\ge  6.\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nSo, for our Laplace transform, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}0\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{2}^{6}1\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{6}^{\\infty }2\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{6}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{6}^{\\infty }2{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the second integral diverges for $s \\leq 0.$\n\nFor $s > 0,$ we calculate our Laplace transform as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{2}^{6}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{6}^{\\infty }2{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{6}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+2\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{6}^{a}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{{e}^{-st}}{s}]}_{2}^{6}+2\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\left(-\\frac{{e}^{-st}}{s}\\right)|}_{6}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-6s}}{s}+\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{s}-\\frac{2}{s}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }({e}^{-sa}-{e}^{-6s}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{s}-\\frac{{e}^{-6s}}{s}-\\frac{2}{s}(0-{e}^{-6s}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{s}-\\frac{{e}^{-6s}}{s}+\\frac{2{e}^{-6s}}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{s}+\\frac{{e}^{-6s}}{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{f(t)\\right\\} =\\dfrac{e^{-2s}}{s} +\\dfrac{e^{-6s}}{s}, \n\\qquad \ns > 0.\n$$"] [17592186229728 :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\mathrm{\\infty }}{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}(nx) \\\\ g(x) & ∼\\frac{{c}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\mathrm{\\infty }}{c}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}(nx)+{d}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}(nx)\\end{aligned}\n$$\nLet $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ be $2π$-periodic extensions of some functions from $x ∈ [- π, π)$ to $x ∈ R$ with the Fourier series shown above. What is the constant Fourier coefficient of $-4f(x) + g(x)$?"] [17592186229729 :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\mathrm{\\infty }}{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}(nx) \\\\ g(x) & ∼\\frac{{c}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\mathrm{\\infty }}{c}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}(nx)+{d}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}(nx)\\end{aligned}\n$$\nLet $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ be $2π$-periodic extensions of some functions from $x ∈ [- π, π)$ to $x ∈ R$ with the Fourier series shown above. What is the $3rd$ Fourier sine coefficient of $-2f(x) + 5g(x)$?"] [17592186229731 :question/problem "Which of the following functions are piecewise continuous on $x\\in [-1,1].$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{x},\\,g(x)=\\begin{cases}0,\\, & x<0 \\\\ 1,\\, & x\\ge 0,\\end{cases}\\,h(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$"] [17592186229735 :question/problem "Use the definition of Laplace transforms to calculate $F(s)=L{f(t)},$ where $f(t)$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(t)=\\begin{cases}{e}^{-2t},\\, & t\\le 3 \\\\ 1\\, & t>3.\\end{cases}\n$$\n\n$\\,F(s)=L{f(t)}=$ {{field-1}},\nvalid for {{field-2}}."] [17592186229735 :question/worked-solution "We apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for the piecewise function \n\n$$\nf(t)=\\begin{cases}{e}^{-2t},\\, & t\\le 3 \\\\ 1\\, & t>3\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{e}^{-2t}\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }1\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{e}^{-2t-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }1\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{e}^{-(2+s)t}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the improper integral diverges if $s\\le 0.$\n\nFor $s>0,$ we calculate our Laplace transform as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{e}^{-(2+s)t}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-(2+s)}{[{e}^{-(2+s)t}]}_{0}^{3}+{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{e}^{-3(2+s)}}{2+s}+\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{3}^{a}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{e}^{-3(2+s)}}{2+s}+\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{-{e}^{-st}}{s}\\right)|}_{3}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{e}^{-3(2+s)}}{2+s}-\\frac{1}{s}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }({e}^{-as}-{e}^{-3s}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{e}^{-3(2+s)}}{2+s}-\\frac{1}{s}\\cdot (0-{e}^{-3s}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{e}^{-3(2+s)}}{2+s}+\\frac{{e}^{-3s}}{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nvalid for $s>0.$"] [17592186230222 :tutorial/content "So far, we have focused on finding the Laplace transforms of functions such as $1$ and $e^{kt}$, which are defined by a single formula.\n\nIn many real-world applications, however, a function is defined by different rules on different time intervals.  \n\nRecall that a function is *piecewise continuous* on $[0,\\infty)$ if it is continuous on each interval of a finite partition, with possible jump discontinuities at the partition points. A basic example is the **unit step function**:\n\n$$\n\\begin{cases}0, & t<0 \\\\ 1, & t\\ge 0.\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nEven though $u(t)$ is defined for all real $t$, notice that the Laplace transform only “sees” values for $t\\ge 0$:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{f(t)\\} = \\int_0^\\infty e^{-st}f(t)\\,\\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nSo, on $[0,\\infty)$ we have $u(t)=1$, and the Laplace transform reduces to\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{u(t)\\} = \\int_0^\\infty e^{-st}\\,\\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nThis improper integral converges exactly when $s>0$, and in that case\n\n$$\n\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\mathcal L\\{u(t)\\} = \\dfrac{1}{s},\\qquad s>0.}\n$$\n\n**Note.** As far as Laplace transforms are concerned, $u(t)$ and the constant function $1$ are *indistinguishable*, because both equal $1$ for all $t\\ge 0$."] [17592186230224 :tutorial/content "As we've seen, the *unit step function* shifted by $a>0$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{cases}0, & t<a, \\\\ 1, & t\\ge a.\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nTo compute its Laplace transform, we apply the definition. Since $u(t-a) = 0$ for $t < a,$ the integral vanishes on the interval $[0,a){:}$\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{u(t-a)\\}\n= \\int_0^\\infty e^{-st}u(t-a)\\,\\textrm d t\n= \\int_a^\\infty e^{-st}\\,\\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nFor $s>0,$ this improper integral converges and evaluates to\n\n$$\n\\int_a^\\infty e^{-st}\\,\\textrm d t\n= \\left[-\\dfrac{e^{-st}}{s}\\right]_a^\\infty\n= \\dfrac{e^{-as}}{s}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the **Laplace transform of the shifted unit step function** is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{u(t-a)\\}\n= \\dfrac{e^{-as}}{s}, \\qquad s>0.\n$$\n\nThis result will be used repeatedly to simplify Laplace transforms of piecewise-defined functions involving step functions."] [17592186230225 :tutorial/content "Along with existence, the Laplace transform also satisfies an important **uniqueness** property.\n\nSuppose $f$ and $g$ are *piecewise continuous* on $[0,\\infty)$ and of exponential order $s_0,$ and that their Laplace transforms\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\}, \\qquad G(s)=\\mathcal{L}\\{g(t)\\}, \\qquad s>s_0,\n$$\n\nsatisfy\n\n$$\nF(s)=G(s) \\quad \\text{for all } s>s_0.\n$$\n\nThen\n\n$$\nf(t)=g(t) \\quad \\text{for almost every  } t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nMoreover, if $f$ and $g$ are continuous, then\n\n$$\nf(t)=g(t) \\quad \\text{for all } t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nTo understand the meaning of *almost every*, consider the following piecewise continuous functions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{cases}0, & t<1, \\\\ 1, & t\\ge 1,\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nThese functions differ only at the single point $t=1.$ Nevertheless,\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{f(t)\\}\n= \\mathcal L\\{g(t)\\}\n= \\mathcal L\\{h(t)\\}\n= \\dfrac{e^{-s}}{s}.\n$$\n\nChanging the value of a piecewise continuous function at finitely or countably many isolated points does not affect its Laplace transform, since the transform is defined using an integral.\n\nIn short, for piecewise continuous functions, the Laplace transform determines the function completely, except for its values at isolated jump points."] [17592186230232 :tutorial/content "When $f(t)$ is defined by different formulas on different intervals, the Laplace transform is computed by **splitting the integral at the breakpoints**.\n\nSuppose $f(t)$ has breakpoints at $t=a$ and $t=b$, where $0 < a < b$, so that\n\n$$\n\\begin{cases}{f}_{1}(t), & 0\\le t<a, \\\\ {f}_{2}(t), & a\\le t<b, \\\\ {f}_{3}(t), & t\\ge b.\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nThen, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{f(t)\\}\n= \\int_0^a e^{-st}f_1(t)\\,\\textrm d t\n+ \\int_a^b e^{-st}f_2(t)\\,\\textrm d t\n+ \\int_b^\\infty e^{-st}f_3(t)\\,\\textrm d t.\n$$\n\n**Key Considerations:**\n\n- **Computation:** Each piece is usually a standard definite or improper integral.\n\n- **Domain:** The Laplace transform exists only for those $s$ where *every improper integral you create is finite*. The final domain is the *intersection* of the domains needed for each piece.\n\nIn this lesson, the only improper integrals we will encounter are of the form\n\n$$\n\\int_c^\\infty e^{(\\alpha-s)t}\\,\\textrm d t,\n$$\n\nwhich converges exactly when $s>\\alpha.$\n\nNext, we will apply this method to a concrete example."] [17592186230243 :tutorial/content "We often want to deal with functions that behave \"nicely\" on a closed interval: \n\n- Often, they should be continuous most of the time, and\n\n- any discontinuities should be simple and well-behaved.\n\nA function $f(x)$ is **piecewise continuous** on $x\\in [a,b]$ if\n\n- it is continuous on $[a,b]$ except at *finitely many points*, and\n\n- at each point of discontinuity, *the one-sided limits exist and are finite*.\n\nIn other words, there can only be finitely many discontinuities, and each of them must be either \n\n- a point discontinuity (also called a removable discontinuity), or\n\n- a jump discontinuity.\n\n**Note:** Every *continuous* function is *piecewise continuous* since it has no discontinuities at all.\n\nLet's consider two examples on the interval $x \\in [0,\\pi].$\n\n- The function\n\n$$\n\\begin{cases}x+2,\\, & x<1 \\\\ 5-x,\\, & x\\ge 1\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nis piecewise continuous on $x\\in[0,\\pi]$ since it is continuous on $x\\in[0,\\pi]$ except at the point $x=1,$ where there is a jump discontinuity and the one-sided limits exist and are finite:\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to1^-}h(x)=3,\n\\qquad\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to1^+}h(x)=4\n$$\n\n![](images/93/9317638fa078685a9a161bf7744142cc3cc51c1b46d6602a73a4b5d0b01be585.png)\n\n- The function\n\n$$\nf(x)=2\\tan x\n$$\n\nis not piecewise continuous on $x\\in[0,\\pi]$ since the one-sided limits at $x=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ are infinite:\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to\\pi/2^-}f(x)=+\\infty,\n\\qquad\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to\\pi/2^+}f(x)=-\\infty\n$$\n\n![](images/6b/6bf9027c9dca2731a4a142bfdea94466aa44f54cc7549691f788c34beed2ce24.png)"] [17592186231372 :question/worked-solution "If each notebook costs $4$ dollars and a student buys $x$ notebooks, then the total cost can be written as\n\n$$\n4x\n$$\n\nBecause the total cost is *less than* $30$ dollars, this quantity must satisfy the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Total cost} & <30 \\\\ 4x & <30\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct inequality is\n\n$$\n4x < 30\n$$"] [17592186231373 :question/worked-solution "Each batch requires\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{4}\n$$\n\nliter of blue dye. If the artist mixes $x$ batches, then the total amount of blue dye used can be written as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{4}x\n$$\n\nBecause the total amount of blue dye used is *less than* $6$ liters, this quantity must satisfy the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Total dye used} & <6 \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}x & <6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct inequality is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{4}x < 6\n$$"] [17592186232036 :tutorial/content "In many real-world situations, we want to describe quantities that must stay *below*, *above*, or *at least* some value.\n\nFor example:\n\n- A budget must stay *below* a certain amount.\n- A factory must produce *at least* a certain number of items.\n- A container must hold *no more than* a certain weight.\n\nSituations like these are modeled using *inequalities*.\n\nFor example, suppose Liam originally had $s$ stickers. He gave $4$ of his stickers to a friend, and now he has fewer than $20$ stickers. Which inequality represents this situation?\n\nWe are told that Liam originally had $s$ stickers.\n\nSince he gave $4$ stickers to a friend, the number of stickers he has now is\n$s - 4$.\n\nBecause Liam now has *fewer than* $20$ stickers, this quantity must satisfy the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Liam's stickers after giving some away} & <20 \\\\ s-4 & <20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct inequality is\n$s - 4 < 20$"] [17592186233020 :question/worked-solution "Here, we apply the quotient rule and the power rule to get\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -1}\\frac{(2{x}^{4}+4)^{2}}{(x-2)^{3}} & =\\frac{\\lim_{x\\to -1}(2{x}^{4}+4)^{2}}{\\lim_{x\\to -1}(x-2)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{(\\lim_{x\\to -1}(2{x}^{4}+4))}^{2}}{{(\\lim_{x\\to -1}(x-2))}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2(-1)^{4}+4)^{2}}{(-1-2)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(6)^{2}}{(-3)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{-27} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186233021 :question/worked-solution "Here, we apply the product rule and the power rule to get\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{y\\to -2}{({y}^{3}+2)}^{3}(2y+3) & =(\\lim_{y\\to -2}({y}^{3}+2)^{3})\\cdot (\\lim_{y\\to -2}(2y+3)) \\\\  & ={(\\lim_{y\\to -2}({y}^{3}+2))}^{3}\\cdot (\\lim_{y\\to -2}(2y+3)) \\\\  & =((-2)^{3}+2)^{3}\\cdot (2\\cdot (-2)+3) \\\\  & =(-6)^{3}\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =216.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186233022 :question/worked-solution "Applying the sum and root rules, we get\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 4}(\\sqrt[3]{({x}^{2}-3x-5)^{5}}-\\sqrt{(2x-7)^{3}}) & =\\lim_{x\\to 4}\\sqrt[3]{({x}^{2}-3x-5)^{5}}-\\lim_{x\\to 4}\\sqrt{(2x-7)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{{(\\lim_{x\\to 4}({x}^{2}-3x-5))}^{5}}-\\sqrt{{(\\lim_{x\\to 4}(2x-7))}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{({4}^{2}-3(4)-5)^{5}}-\\sqrt{(2(4)-7)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{(16-12-5)^{5}}-\\sqrt{(8-7)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{(-1)^{5}}-\\sqrt{(1)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{-1}-\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =-1-1 \\\\  & =-2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186233023 :question/worked-solution "From the graph, we get that $\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=1.$ \n\nFrom the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}(2{x}^{3}-4f(x))^{3}(2{x}^{4}-3)^{2}=\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}(2{x}^{3}-4f(x))^{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(2{x}^{4}-3)^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe calculate the limits of each factor, separately.\n\nApplying the power rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}(2{x}^{3}-4f(x))^{3} & ={(\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}(2{x}^{3}-4f(x)))}^{3} \\\\  & =(2(1)^{3}-4(1))^{3} \\\\  & =(-2)^{3} \\\\  & =-8,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}(2{x}^{4}-3)^{2} & ={(\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}(2{x}^{4}-3))}^{2} \\\\  & =(2(1)^{4}-3)^{2} \\\\  & =(-1)^{2} \\\\  & =1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}(2{x}^{3}-4f(x))^{3}(2{x}^{4}-3)^{2} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}(2{x}^{3}-4f(x))^{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(2{x}^{4}-3)^{2} \\\\  & =(-8)\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =-8\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186233024 :question/worked-solution "From the graph we get that $\\lim_{x\\to -2}f(x)=2.$ \n\nFrom the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -2}({x}^{3}+4)^{2}(4-2f(x))^{5}=\\lim_{x\\to -2}({x}^{3}+4)^{2}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -2}(4-2f(x))^{5}.\n$$\n\nWe calculate the limits of each factor, separately.\n\nApplying the power rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -2}({x}^{3}+4)^{2} & ={(\\lim_{x\\to -2}({x}^{3}+4))}^{2} \\\\  & ={((-2)^{3}+4)}^{2} \\\\  & ={(-8+4)}^{2} \\\\  & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -2}(4-2f(x))^{5} & ={(\\lim_{x\\to -2}(4-2f(x)))}^{5} \\\\  & ={(4-2(2))}^{5} \\\\  & ={(4-4)}^{5} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -2}({x}^{3}+4)^{2}(4-2f(x))^{5} & ={(\\lim_{x\\to -2}({x}^{3}+4))}^{2}{(\\lim_{x\\to -2}(4-2f(x)))}^{5} \\\\  & =16\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186233025 :question/worked-solution "From the graph, we get that $\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=2.$ \n\nFrom the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}(1-3x)^{3}(3f(x)-4x)^{2}=\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}(1-3x)^{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(3f(x)-4x)^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe calculate the limits of each factor, separately.\n\nApplying the power rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}(1-3x)^{3} & ={(\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}(1-3x))}^{3} \\\\  & =(1-3(1))^{3} \\\\  & =(-2)^{3} \\\\  & =-8,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}(3f(x)-4x)^{2} & ={(\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}(3f(x)-4x))}^{2} \\\\  & =(3(2)-4(1))^{2} \\\\  & ={2}^{2} \\\\  & =4\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}(1-3x)^{3}(3f(x)-4x)^{2} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}(1-3x)^{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(3f(x)-4x)^{2} \\\\  & =(-8)\\cdot (4) \\\\  & =-32\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186233026 :question/worked-solution "From the graph, we get that $\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=3.$ \n\nFrom the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}(3-x)^{3}(f(x)-4{x}^{2})^{5}=\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}(3-x)^{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(f(x)-4{x}^{2})^{5}.\n$$\n\nWe calculate the limits of each factor, separately.\n\nApplying the power rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}(3-x)^{3} & ={(\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}(3-x))}^{3} \\\\  & =(3-1)^{3} \\\\  & ={2}^{3} \\\\  & =8,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}(f(x)-4{x}^{2})^{4} & ={(\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}(f(x)-4{x}^{2}))}^{5} \\\\  & =(3-4(1)^{2})^{5} \\\\  & =(-1)^{5} \\\\  & =-1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}(3-x)^{3}(f(x)-4{x}^{2})^{5} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}(3-x)^{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(f(x)-4{x}^{2})^{5} \\\\  & =8\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-8\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186233029 :question/worked-solution "From the graph, we get that  $\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=1.$ \n\nApplying the root rule, we get\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}\\frac{3f(x)+1}{\\sqrt{f(x)+3}} & =\\frac{\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}(3f(x)+1)}{\\sqrt{\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}(f(x)+3)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(1)+1}{\\sqrt{(1)+3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{4}} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186233047 :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{\\sqrt{4x-3}-3}{x-3} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{4(3)-3}-3}{3-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{9}-3}{3-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{\\sqrt{4x-3}-3}{x-3} & =\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{(\\sqrt{4x-3}-3)(\\sqrt{4x-3}+3)}{(x-3)(\\sqrt{4x-3}+3)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{(\\sqrt{4x-3})^{2}-{3}^{2}}{(x-3)(\\sqrt{4x-3}+3)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{4x-3-9}{(x-3)(\\sqrt{4x-3}+3)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{4x-12}{(x-3)(\\sqrt{4x-3}+3)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{4(x-3)}{(x-3)(\\sqrt{4x-3}+3)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{4x-3}+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{4(3)-3}+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{9}+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186233048 :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{\\sqrt{5x+6}-4}{x-2} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5(2)+6}-4}{2-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{16}-4}{2-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{\\sqrt{5x+6}-4}{x-2} & =\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{(\\sqrt{5x+6}-4)(\\sqrt{5x+6}+4)}{(x-2)(\\sqrt{5x+6}+4)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{(\\sqrt{5x+6})^{2}-{4}^{2}}{(x-2)(\\sqrt{5x+6}+4)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{5x+6-16}{(x-2)(\\sqrt{5x+6}+4)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{5x-10}{(x-2)(\\sqrt{5x+6}+4)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{5(x-2)}{(x-2)(\\sqrt{5x+6}+4)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{5x+6}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{5(2)+6}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{16}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186233049 :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit by simply evaluating the function at $x=0,$ we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3x+4}-2} & =\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{3(0)+4}-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{4}-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the denominator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3x+4}-2} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x(\\sqrt{3x+4}+2)}{(\\sqrt{3x+4}-2)(\\sqrt{3x+4}+2)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x(\\sqrt{3x+4}+2)}{(\\sqrt{3x+4})^{2}-{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x(\\sqrt{3x+4}+2)}{3x+4-4} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x(\\sqrt{3x+4}+2)}{3x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\sqrt{3x+4}+2}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3(0)+4}+2}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{4}+2}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2+2}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186233067 :question/worked-solution "*Method 1:*\n\nBy the definition, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(3)=\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(3+h)-f(3)}{h}.\n$$\n\nApplying this to $f(x)=10{x}^{2}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(3) & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{10(3+h)^{2}-10(3)^{2}}{h}\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{90+60h+10{h}^{2}-90}{h}\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{60h+10{h}^{2}}{h}\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}(60+10h) \\\\  & =60+(0) \\\\  & =60.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n*Method 2:*\n\nBy the definition, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(3)=\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{f(x)-f(3)}{x-3}.\n$$\n\nApplying this to $f(x)=10{x}^{2}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(3) & =\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\left(\\frac{10{x}^{2}-10(3)^{2}}{x-3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\left(\\frac{10({x}^{2}-{3}^{2})}{x-3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\left(\\frac{10(x-3)(x+3)}{x-3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 3}10(x+3) \\\\  & =10(3+3) \\\\  & =60.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186233080 :question/problem "For what reason does the function $f(x)$ given below have a discontinuity at $x=\\sqrt{2}?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}-\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}-2}, & x<\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\frac{4}{{x}^{2}-2}, & x>\\sqrt{2}\\end{cases}\n$$"]}
